      "editorData": "<h3 style=\"color:#e8eaed;font-size:15px;margin:0 0 8px\">Hardware refresh &mdash; sizing new hosts</h3>\n\n<p style=\"color:#9aa4b0;margin:0 0 9px;line-height:1.5\">This screen answers <b style=\"color:#e8eaed\">how much RAM do we actually need to buy</b>. Installed DRAM\nis an <i>output</i> here: size presented memory to the workload, then split it DRAM/NVMe by the\nratio the workload can carry. The companion screen,\n<b style=\"color:#e8eaed\">Memory Tier Advanced &mdash; Clusters</b>, answers the other case: keeping the hosts you\nown and adding NVMe.</p>\n\n<p style=\"color:#9aa4b0;margin:0 0 9px;line-height:1.5\"><b style=\"color:#e8eaed\">Read these columns, in this order.</b>\n<b style=\"color:#e8eaed\">Allocated (powered on)</b> is what the new hosts must present &mdash; not the raw\nallocated figure, which counts powered-off VMs and templates. &middot;\n<b style=\"color:#e8eaed\">DRAM floor</b> is peak active: the least DRAM that holds the working set. &middot;\n<b style=\"color:#e8eaed\">DRAM conservative</b> is the planning number. &middot;\n<b style=\"color:#e8eaed\">RAM saved</b> is what you do not buy, versus an untiered refresh where every presented\nGB must be DRAM.</p>\n\n<p style=\"color:#9aa4b0;margin:0 0 9px;line-height:1.5\"><b style=\"color:#e8b84b\">Subtract pinned memory before quoting anything.</b> <b style=\"color:#e8eaed\">Pinned GB</b> is\nmemory belonging to VMs that cannot be tiered at all &mdash; 1 GB pages, latency-sensitive,\nlocked reservations, PCI passthrough or vGPU, or fully reserved. That memory must be DRAM in\nany design, so the real saving is the saving column <i>less</i> the pinned column.\n<b style=\"color:#e8eaed\">Tierable GB</b> does that subtraction for you.</p>\n\n<p style=\"color:#9aa4b0;margin:0 0 9px;line-height:1.5\"><b style=\"color:#e8b84b\">And check CPU before believing the memory answer.</b> New hosts sized on memory\nalone will be wrong if CPU binds first &mdash; compare <b style=\"color:#e8eaed\">CPU headroom %</b> against\n<b style=\"color:#e8eaed\">Active of host RAM %</b>. Ready time that is fixable by rightsizing frees CPU and moves\nthe constraint back to memory, which is where tiering pays.</p>\n\n<p style=\"color:#9aa4b0;margin:0 0 9px;line-height:1.5\">Everything here reads the <b style=\"color:#e8eaed\">Maximum</b> over the dashboard time range, so set it\nto a full business cycle. A 24-hour window sizes a purchase off a quiet Tuesday.</p>",
